<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suetonius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 69–after 122</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Gaius Suetonius Tranquillus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suetonius was a Roman biographer and imperial official who served as a secretary to the emperors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> and Hadrian. His Lives of the Twelve Caesars, covering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a>, is a main source for the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and records details such as Vespasian's campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. Many of his other writings, on grammarians, poets and other subjects, survive only in part.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suetonius.jpg" alt="Suetonius" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Achille Réveil · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suetonius_etching_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Suetonius was probably born about 69 CE, a date deduced from his remark that he was about 57 years after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>'s death. His place of birth is disputed, but most scholars place it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a>, a small town in Numidia in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>. His family was of moderate social position, and his father, Suetonius Laetus, was a tribune of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/equites">equestrian order</a> in Legio XIII Gemina. He was educated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> at a time when schools of rhetoric flourished.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Suetonius was a close friend of the senator and letter-writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-younger">Pliny the Younger</a>, who described him as quiet and studious, a man dedicated to writing. Pliny helped him buy a small property and interceded with the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> to grant him the tax immunities given to a father of three, although he was childless. Through Pliny he came into favour with Trajan and Hadrian. Under Trajan he was secretary of studies and director of the imperial archives, and under Hadrian he became the emperor's secretary. The Historia Augusta, an often unreliable collection written more than 150 years later, says that Hadrian dismissed him for acting too informally around the empress Vibia Sabina.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His best-known surviving work is De vita Caesarum, the Twelve Caesars, a collective biography of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, Augustus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galba">Galba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otho">Otho</a>, Vitellius, Vespasian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-saint">Titus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a>. It was probably written in Hadrian's time and dedicated to his friend Gaius Septicius Clarus, prefect of the Praetorian Guard in 119. Each life follows a set formula, giving appearance, omens, family history and quotations before the history, and the first chapters on Caesar are missing. It contains the earliest accounts of Caesar's epileptic seizures.</p><p>Other works dealt with the daily life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, politics, oratory and the lives of writers. His Lives of the Grammarians survives, apparently complete, with 20 brief lives, and five of the sixteen Lives of the Rhetoricians survive. From the Lives of the Poets come the life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> and fragments of the lives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terence">Terence</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horace">Horace</a> and Lucan. Many of his books are lost.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Suetonius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Suetonius</b> records that Vespasian took away the freedom of Byzantium, along with that of Achaea, Lycia, Rhodes and Samos, and around 73 CE it was incorporated into a Roman province.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Cunobelinus, ruling from about 9 to 40 CE from Camulodunum, which he took from the Trinovantes, extended influence over Kent and much of the south-east; <b>Suetonius</b> called him king of the Britons.</blockquote><a href="/state/catuvellauni">Catuvellauni</a></li><li><blockquote>In 43 and 44 the future emperor Vespasian, leading the legio II Augusta, took more than twenty strongholds in the south-west according to <b>Suetonius</b>; a Roman fort was built inside Hod Hill.</blockquote><a href="/state/durotriges">Durotriges</a></li></ul></section>
